= Relation =

== Overview ==
A '''relation''' is a connection that holds among two or more things.

Examples:
* "Alice is parent of Bob";
* "Paris is located in France";
* "5 is greater than 3";
* "Module A depends on Module B".

Relations are central to logic, mathematics, [[Ontology|ontology]], databases and [[Knowledge representation|knowledge representation]].

== Arity ==
The number of arguments a relation connects is its '''arity'''.

A unary predicate concerns one thing.

A binary relation connects two things.

A ternary relation connects three.

Examples:

<pre>
ParentOf(Alice, Bob)
Between(A, B, C)
</pre>

Many knowledge systems emphasize binary relations because graphs represent them naturally as edges.

== Relation type and relation instance ==
A relation type such as "parent of" is different from one particular relation instance such as:

<pre>
Alice parentOf Bob
</pre>

The first gives the kind of connection.

The second asserts that the connection holds between specific relata.

== Mathematical relation ==
In mathematics, an n-ary relation can be represented extensionally as a set of n-tuples.

For binary relation R over sets A and B:

<pre>
R ⊆ A × B
</pre>

This extensional view specifies exactly which ordered pairs stand in the relation.

Other theories also care about intensional meaning: what the relation means, not only which pairs currently satisfy it.

== Relation and property ==
A [[Property]] can be treated as a special kind of relation in some formalisms.

For example, "hasColor" relates an object to a color value.

Philosophically, properties and relations are sometimes distinguished by arity or metaphysical role.

In RDF, a property is specifically used as the predicate of a binary subject–object relation.

The terms therefore overlap without being universally identical.

== Direction ==
Many relations are directional.

<pre>
A dependsOn B
</pre>

does not mean:

<pre>
B dependsOn A
</pre>

Direction should be explicit when the inverse relation has a different meaning.

== Symmetry ==
A relation R is symmetric when:

<pre>
R(a,b) → R(b,a)
</pre>

"Is sibling of" is often modeled symmetrically.

"Is parent of" is not.

Symmetry is a semantic characteristic and should not be assumed from natural-language appearance.

== Transitivity ==
A relation is transitive when:

<pre>
R(a,b) ∧ R(b,c) → R(a,c)
</pre>

"Greater than" is transitive.

Many real-world relations are not.

Generic "causes", "related to" or "supports" should not automatically be made transitive.

== Inverses ==
A relation can have an inverse.

<pre>
parentOf ↔ childOf
</pre>

A system may store one direction and derive the other for display or query.

Derived inverse presentation is not necessarily a second stored fact.

== Domain and range ==
A relation can declare intended source and target types.

For example:

<pre>
bornIn:
  domain Person
  range Place
</pre>

In formal languages such as RDFS, domain/range statements can trigger inference rather than act as ordinary validation constraints.

This distinction matters.

== Part–whole relations ==
"Part of" is not one simple relation in every domain.

Physical componenthood, membership, ingredient, phase, region and organizational inclusion can obey different rules.

Mereology studies part–whole structure formally.

A knowledge graph should avoid pretending all forms of "part of" support identical transitive inference.

== Causal relations ==
[[Causality|Causal relations]] connect causes and effects.

They are stronger than association and may be conditional, probabilistic or mediated.

Encoding a "causes" edge is therefore an epistemic claim requiring evidence, not merely a graph-design choice.

== Relationships in knowledge graphs ==
In a [[Knowledge graph]], relations are often represented as labeled edges.

The usefulness of the graph depends heavily on relation semantics.

A graph where every edge is simply "related to" can aid navigation but supports little precise reasoning.

Specific, documented edge meanings improve queries and inference.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki defines a small local set of relations at [[BITwiki:Relationships]] such as Implements, Part of, Depends on, Supports, Contradicts, Refines and Derived from.

Those are project-specific relation types, not a universal list.

